<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Boyington asked for a same-day visit for low back pain. Two days ago, while helping a friend move, he lifted a heavy box with his back bent and felt a sudden pull across the left lower back. The pain is a dull ache with sharp catches when he bends, twists, or gets out of a chair, and it is easing a little each day. It does not travel into the legs. He has no numbness, tingling, or weakness, and no change in bladder or bowel control or numbness between the legs. He has had no fever, weight loss beyond the expected loss since he cut down on drinking, or prior back injury. Ice and walking help. The pain woke him on both nights, and on the second night he had a nightmare for the first time in about two weeks. He felt an urge to have a drink to get back to sleep, did not, and called a peer from his recovery group instead. He has not drunk alcohol since his review last week. He has taken two doses of ibuprofen 400 mg. A friend offered him leftover muscle relaxant tablets, which he declined and asked about today. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, lifting a heavy box with his back bent</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Left lower back; no radiation into the legs</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days, easing slightly each day</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Dull ache with sharp catches on bending, twisting, and standing up</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; 6 of 10 at worst, 3 of 10 at rest; woke him both nights</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Worse with movement and after sitting; stiff in the morning</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Helping a friend move; no prior back injury</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Ice and walking help; two doses of ibuprofen 400 mg; no alcohol</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>One nightmare and a craving on the second night, managed with his relapse plan. Denies leg pain, numbness, weakness, bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, and fever</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

