  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute lumbar muscle strain, without nerve involvement or red flags (S39.012A)</content>. Mechanical low back pain after a lifting injury, with a normal neurological examination and no red flags such as bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, fever, cancer history, or major trauma. Imaging is not indicated. Most acute strains improve within a few weeks. [S]<list><item>Stay active and walk; avoid bed rest. Superficial heat or ice for 15 to 20 minutes several times a day.</item><item>Ibuprofen 400 mg up to three times a day with food for up to seven days; take it only on days without alcohol, because the combination raises the risk of stomach bleeding.</item><item>If ibuprofen upsets his stomach, acetaminophen up to 2 g a day instead, kept at that lower limit because of his drinking history.</item><item>No muscle relaxants or opioids. Both cause sedation that adds to alcohol, and the leftover tablets from a friend should not be taken; he agrees.</item><item>Lift with the knees and hold loads close to the body; gentle back exercises handout given.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder in early recovery (F10.20)</content>. Pain and broken sleep triggered a craving, which he managed with his relapse plan. No drinking since last week&apos;s review. [S]<list><item>Keep no alcohol in the house while the pain disturbs his sleep and continue the peer group.</item><item>Call the clinic or his peer contact if the cravings return.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, improved (F43.10)</content>. A single nightmare after a pain-broken night, against his recent pattern. No suicidal thoughts; the safety plan remains current. [S]<list><item>Use the sleep routine from therapy; a booster session with Dr. Lee is available if nightmares become more frequent.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Low Back Pain (2022)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in about three weeks, where the back will be rechecked. Return sooner for pain spreading below the knee, leg numbness or weakness, any bladder or bowel change, numbness between the legs, or fever. Call or use the crisis line, 988 then 1, at any time. Next scheduled visit 1957-10-28: adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
